{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}\\times \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\times 1}{10\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 3}{10\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times \\cancel{3}}{10\\times \\cancel{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate in this situation is $\\frac{3}{10}$ gallons per minute." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186150389 :answer-field/correct 17592186150391 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-06090a54-3bf7-5dce-b7f7-51954f025453" :question/id #uuid "06090a54-3bf7-5dce-b7f7-51954f025453" :question/math-academy-id "q-71434" :question/problem "A container with $\\frac{3}{4}$ gallons of weed killer can spray $\\frac{7}{4}$ lawns. What is the unit rate of gallons per lawn?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the unit rate of gallons per lawn, we divide the number of gallons, $\\frac{3}{4},$ by the number of lawns, $\\frac{7}{4}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{7}{4} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\times \\frac{4}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 4}{4\\times 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times \\cancel{4}}{\\cancel{4}\\times 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $\\frac{3}{7}$ gallons per lawn." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-09020a00-7821-5c7f-a323-c2393071bde1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-09020a00-7821-5c7f-a323-c2393071bde1" :answer-field/id #uuid "09020a00-7821-5c7f-a323-c2393071bde1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7ebc5524-a707-5058-b0e2-7b06a1664d65" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16a97f78-f4fe-5855-b81b-381b3a37c9e7" "answer-1005ee30-9311-5fb9-a232-e0178b92429a" "answer-7ebc5524-a707-5058-b0e2-7b06a1664d65" "answer-ff687e65-7e6f-5e64-a0f5-de85e7a030e7" "answer-01f2bbf6-d10e-547b-9978-414fb88af65f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16a97f78-f4fe-5855-b81b-381b3a37c9e7" :answer/id #uuid "16a97f78-f4fe-5855-b81b-381b3a37c9e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ gallons per lawn" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1005ee30-9311-5fb9-a232-e0178b92429a" :answer/id #uuid "1005ee30-9311-5fb9-a232-e0178b92429a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$7$ gallons per lawn" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ebc5524-a707-5058-b0e2-7b06a1664d65" :answer/id #uuid "7ebc5524-a707-5058-b0e2-7b06a1664d65" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{7}$ gallons per lawn" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff687e65-7e6f-5e64-a0f5-de85e7a030e7" :answer/id #uuid "ff687e65-7e6f-5e64-a0f5-de85e7a030e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{7}$ gallons per lawn" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01f2bbf6-d10e-547b-9978-414fb88af65f" :answer/id #uuid "01f2bbf6-d10e-547b-9978-414fb88af65f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{7}{3}$ gallons per lawn" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6cccdce8-916f-545e-8523-5e2c1794424a" :question/id #uuid "6cccdce8-916f-545e-8523-5e2c1794424a" :question/math-academy-id "q-67967" :question/problem "Riding his electric bike, Matt can go $\\frac{1}{2}$ of a mile in $\\frac{3}{4}$  minutes. What is the unit rate of miles covered per minute?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the unit rate of miles per minute, we divide the number of miles, $\\frac{1}{2},$ by the number of minutes, $\\frac{3}{4}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}÷\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}÷\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 4}{2\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2}{2\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\times 2}{\\cancel{2}\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $\\frac{2}{3}$ miles per minute." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55" :answer-field/id #uuid "80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-08d6df6d-3090-5380-af3f-b6a2c1297623" :answer-field/choices ["answer-34d51373-e059-5bb2-b915-ea1bf3300d5d" "answer-08d6df6d-3090-5380-af3f-b6a2c1297623" "answer-d6bb8ba7-cf6b-5bb3-aa7f-0dc97af0d2b1" "answer-4bc6dd10-ce27-56ea-8697-58814869e5a1" "answer-a26136a8-2a89-5671-9dc1-9250849e70bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34d51373-e059-5bb2-b915-ea1bf3300d5d" :answer/id #uuid "34d51373-e059-5bb2-b915-ea1bf3300d5d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{2}$ mile per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08d6df6d-3090-5380-af3f-b6a2c1297623" :answer/id #uuid "08d6df6d-3090-5380-af3f-b6a2c1297623" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{2}{3}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6bb8ba7-cf6b-5bb3-aa7f-0dc97af0d2b1" :answer/id #uuid "d6bb8ba7-cf6b-5bb3-aa7f-0dc97af0d2b1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{4}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bc6dd10-ce27-56ea-8697-58814869e5a1" :answer/id #uuid "4bc6dd10-ce27-56ea-8697-58814869e5a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{4}{5}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a26136a8-2a89-5671-9dc1-9250849e70bc" :answer/id #uuid "a26136a8-2a89-5671-9dc1-9250849e70bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{6}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-37159145-3e5f-53f2-822d-4367df7cb7bc" :question/id #uuid "37159145-3e5f-53f2-822d-4367df7cb7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-67957" :question/problem "A skydiver falls $\\frac{4}{5}$ of a mile in $\\frac{2}{5}$ minutes. What is the unit rate of miles fallen per minute?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the unit rate of miles per minute, we divide the number of miles, $\\frac{4}{5},$ by the number of minutes, $\\frac{2}{5}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{2}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\times 5}{5\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2\\times 5}{5\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\times \\cancel{5}\\times 2}{\\cancel{2}\\times \\cancel{5}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $2$ miles per minute." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840" :answer-field/id #uuid "f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a70b8aba-2006-588a-9b36-9664101c1dc6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a70b8aba-2006-588a-9b36-9664101c1dc6" "answer-d42f7600-375a-59ca-9455-2853caf93570" "answer-70090ca7-e591-51bc-b92a-43641ff66542" "answer-493940a9-253b-5a45-a88e-7e139a8230c4" "answer-d43afe7e-a4ce-5422-b4f9-1126acce88dd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a70b8aba-2006-588a-9b36-9664101c1dc6" :answer/id #uuid "a70b8aba-2006-588a-9b36-9664101c1dc6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d42f7600-375a-59ca-9455-2853caf93570" :answer/id #uuid "d42f7600-375a-59ca-9455-2853caf93570" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{10}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70090ca7-e591-51bc-b92a-43641ff66542" :answer/id #uuid "70090ca7-e591-51bc-b92a-43641ff66542" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{2}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-493940a9-253b-5a45-a88e-7e139a8230c4" :answer/id #uuid "493940a9-253b-5a45-a88e-7e139a8230c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{10}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d43afe7e-a4ce-5422-b4f9-1126acce88dd" :answer/id #uuid "d43afe7e-a4ce-5422-b4f9-1126acce88dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{4}{5}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6aea863a-357f-52de-8ad7-b9c9c32993ae" :question/id #uuid "6aea863a-357f-52de-8ad7-b9c9c32993ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-67962" :question/problem "A certain satellite makes $\\frac{1}{3}$  of its orbit around the Earth in $\\frac{1}{6}$ of a day. How many times can it orbit Earth in $1$ day?" :question/worked-solution "To find how many  times the satellite  can go around the Earth in $1$ day, we can compute the orbital unit rate per day. We do this by dividing the amount of the orbit the satellite travels, $\\frac{1}{3},$ by the corresponding fraction of a day, $\\frac{1}{6},$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{6}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 6}{3\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the satellite can orbit Earth $2$ times in $1$ day." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5" :answer-field/id #uuid "3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-41a64a33-32be-5dd3-a470-cc04307cda2b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-41a64a33-32be-5dd3-a470-cc04307cda2b" "answer-506250f5-ba3b-5016-bdc4-5b2f75606b96" "answer-8d0a7fb2-745a-5a4b-8c76-c522f2f54c2a" "answer-663127ee-ca61-54fd-bd7a-e8c3c21cf07f" "answer-926c20da-babe-5216-892f-00c1074d45bb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41a64a33-32be-5dd3-a470-cc04307cda2b" :answer/id #uuid "41a64a33-32be-5dd3-a470-cc04307cda2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-506250f5-ba3b-5016-bdc4-5b2f75606b96" :answer/id #uuid "506250f5-ba3b-5016-bdc4-5b2f75606b96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d0a7fb2-745a-5a4b-8c76-c522f2f54c2a" :answer/id #uuid "8d0a7fb2-745a-5a4b-8c76-c522f2f54c2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-663127ee-ca61-54fd-bd7a-e8c3c21cf07f" :answer/id #uuid "663127ee-ca61-54fd-bd7a-e8c3c21cf07f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-926c20da-babe-5216-892f-00c1074d45bb" :answer/id #uuid "926c20da-babe-5216-892f-00c1074d45bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152651 :question/worked-solution "To find the number of miles Jim can run in $1$ hour, we can compute the unit rate of miles per hour. We do this by dividing the number of miles, $\\frac{5}{6},$ by the number of hours, $\\frac{1}{5},$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{6}÷\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{6}÷\\frac{1}{5} & =\\frac{5}{6}\\times \\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 5}{6\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{6}=4\\,\\text{R}1=4\\,\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jim can run $4\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ miles in $1$ hour." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186150413 :answer-field/correct "answer-148f688d-3a85-57a5-b966-a8820f8f1a74" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d04bb74e-c21f-56f1-9310-a6adde831b6a" "answer-0153e8af-7940-50ee-90ef-b1dfd0e45b5a" "answer-148f688d-3a85-57a5-b966-a8820f8f1a74"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d04bb74e-c21f-56f1-9310-a6adde831b6a" :answer/id #uuid "d04bb74e-c21f-56f1-9310-a6adde831b6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,\\frac{3}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0153e8af-7940-50ee-90ef-b1dfd0e45b5a" :answer/id #uuid "0153e8af-7940-50ee-90ef-b1dfd0e45b5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-148f688d-3a85-57a5-b966-a8820f8f1a74" :answer/id #uuid "148f688d-3a85-57a5-b966-a8820f8f1a74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153341 :tutorial/content "A **unit rate** is a ratio of two quantities stated as some amount of one quantity per unit of the other quantity.\n\nFor example, suppose that Carl can run $100$ meters in $20$ seconds. The unit rate is how many meters Carl can run in $1$ second. To compute Carl's unit rate, we divide the number of meters by the number of seconds:\n\n$$\n100 \\div 20 = 5\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $5$ meters per second. In other words, Carl can run $5$ meters in $1$ second.\n\nUnit rates do not always come out to whole numbers. For example, suppose Jamie can run $100$ meters in $15$ seconds. To compute Jamie's unit rate, we again divide the number of meters by the number of seconds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100÷15 & =\\frac{100}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{100÷5}{15÷5} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{3} = 6 \\, \\textrm{R} 2 = 6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jamie can run $6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}$ meters per second." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152308 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186151914 :question/worked-solution "To compute the unit rate of miles per gallon of gas, we divide the number of miles, $15,$ by the number of gallons of gas, $\\dfrac{3}{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n15 \\div \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15÷\\frac{3}{4} & =15\\times \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\times 4}{1\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{3} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $20$ miles per gallon of gas."] [17592186151915 :question/worked-solution "To find the number of tablespoons in $1$ gram of olive oil, we can compute the unit rate of tablespoons per gram olive oil. We do this by dividing the number of tablespoons, $\\dfrac{1}{3},$ by the number of grams, $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\div 4\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}÷4 & =\\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 1}{3\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is $\\dfrac{1}{12}$ of a tablespoon in $1$ gram of olive oil."] [17592186151916 :question/worked-solution "To find the number of miles that Martha can swim in $1$ hour, we can compute the unit rate of miles per hour. We do this by dividing the number of miles, $\\dfrac{3}{4},$ by the number of hours, $\\dfrac{2}{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} \\div \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{2}{3} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 3}{4\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{8} = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} 1 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Martha can swim $1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8}$ miles in $1$ hour."] ["question-fab8d7a2-8042-5e4e-8fd9-ae407a4d21d4" :question/id #uuid "fab8d7a2-8042-5e4e-8fd9-ae407a4d21d4"] ["question-fab8d7a2-8042-5e4e-8fd9-ae407a4d21d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-71430"] ["question-fab8d7a2-8042-5e4e-8fd9-ae407a4d21d4" :question/problem "Suzie takes $\\frac{2}{3}$ hours to read $22$ pages of a certain book. How many pages can she read in $1$ hour?"] ["question-fab8d7a2-8042-5e4e-8fd9-ae407a4d21d4" :question/worked-solution "To find the number of pages Suzie can read in $1$ hour, we compute the unit rate of pages per hour. We do this by dividing the number of pages Suzie reads, $22,$ by the number of hours, $\\frac{2}{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n22÷\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22÷\\frac{2}{3} & =22\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{22\\times 3}{1\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{66}{2} \\\\  & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Suzie can read $33$ pages in $1$ hour."] ["question-fab8d7a2-8042-5e4e-8fd9-ae407a4d21d4" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-2744fc75-9aee-5472-b571-e924eb38f9e9" :answer-field/id #uuid "2744fc75-9aee-5472-b571-e924eb38f9e9"] ["answer-7331676a-b6b0-50c8-abb5-bc1399ef4d38" :answer/id #uuid "7331676a-b6b0-50c8-abb5-bc1399ef4d38"] ["answer-7331676a-b6b0-50c8-abb5-bc1399ef4d38" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-7331676a-b6b0-50c8-abb5-bc1399ef4d38" :answer/value "$30$ pages"] ["answer-b1c7732a-c197-5e0d-8737-34107092414f" :answer/id #uuid "b1c7732a-c197-5e0d-8737-34107092414f"] ["answer-b1c7732a-c197-5e0d-8737-34107092414f" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-b1c7732a-c197-5e0d-8737-34107092414f" :answer/value "$31\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ pages"] ["answer-72410a5d-4ed2-589c-9a96-915c6691fadf" :answer/id #uuid "72410a5d-4ed2-589c-9a96-915c6691fadf"] ["answer-72410a5d-4ed2-589c-9a96-915c6691fadf" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-72410a5d-4ed2-589c-9a96-915c6691fadf" :answer/value "$32\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ pages"] ["answer-95fb0629-d415-573c-9722-1d179bf8863b" :answer/id #uuid "95fb0629-d415-573c-9722-1d179bf8863b"] ["answer-95fb0629-d415-573c-9722-1d179bf8863b" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-95fb0629-d415-573c-9722-1d179bf8863b" :answer/value "$33$ pages"] ["answer-00dc0803-4289-5d10-9911-65583cfc3728" :answer/id #uuid "00dc0803-4289-5d10-9911-65583cfc3728"] ["answer-00dc0803-4289-5d10-9911-65583cfc3728" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-00dc0803-4289-5d10-9911-65583cfc3728" :answer/value "$38$ pages"] ["answer-field-2744fc75-9aee-5472-b571-e924eb38f9e9" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-2744fc75-9aee-5472-b571-e924eb38f9e9" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-2744fc75-9aee-5472-b571-e924eb38f9e9" :answer-field/correct "answer-95fb0629-d415-573c-9722-1d179bf8863b"] ["answer-field-2744fc75-9aee-5472-b571-e924eb38f9e9" :answer-field/choices "answer-7331676a-b6b0-50c8-abb5-bc1399ef4d38"] ["answer-field-2744fc75-9aee-5472-b571-e924eb38f9e9" :answer-field/choices "answer-b1c7732a-c197-5e0d-8737-34107092414f"] ["answer-field-2744fc75-9aee-5472-b571-e924eb38f9e9" :answer-field/choices "answer-72410a5d-4ed2-589c-9a96-915c6691fadf"] ["answer-field-2744fc75-9aee-5472-b571-e924eb38f9e9" :answer-field/choices "answer-95fb0629-d415-573c-9722-1d179bf8863b"] ["answer-field-2744fc75-9aee-5472-b571-e924eb38f9e9" :answer-field/choices "answer-00dc0803-4289-5d10-9911-65583cfc3728"] ["question-fab8d7a2-8042-5e4e-8fd9-ae407a4d21d4" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-2744fc75-9aee-5472-b571-e924eb38f9e9"] [17592186152104 :knowledge-point/questions "question-fab8d7a2-8042-5e4e-8fd9-ae407a4d21d4"] [17592186152646 :question/worked-solution "To compute the unit rate of feet per second, we divide the number of feet, $40,$ by the number of seconds, $\\frac{5}{6}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n40÷\\frac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40÷\\frac{5}{6} & =40\\times \\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{1}\\times \\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{40\\times 6}{1\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{240}{5} \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate in this situation is $48$ feet per second."] [17592186152646 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] [17592186150383 :answer-field/correct 17592186150384] ["question-c922cf86-366d-5b96-b6c8-e7d64dc90ff6" :question/id #uuid "c922cf86-366d-5b96-b6c8-e7d64dc90ff6"] ["question-c922cf86-366d-5b96-b6c8-e7d64dc90ff6" :question/math-academy-id "q-67905"] ["question-c922cf86-366d-5b96-b6c8-e7d64dc90ff6" :question/problem "A leaking paddling pool loses $6$ liters of water in  $\\frac{2}{3}$ hours. How much water will it lose in $1$ hour?"] ["question-c922cf86-366d-5b96-b6c8-e7d64dc90ff6" :question/worked-solution "To find out how much water the pool will lose in $1$ hour, we can compute the unit rate of liters of water lost per hour. We do this by dividing  the number of liters, $6,$ by the number of hours, $\\frac{2}{3}$, as follows:     \n\n$$\n6÷\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6÷\\frac{2}{3} & =6\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\times 3}{1\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the pool will lose $9$ liters of water in $1$ hour."] ["question-c922cf86-366d-5b96-b6c8-e7d64dc90ff6" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-fd0af01a-73e4-519b-b081-ca37b5a21db0" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd0af01a-73e4-519b-b081-ca37b5a21db0"] ["answer-0f040a93-6f4b-5c58-ad19-03b03ef0421d" :answer/id #uuid "0f040a93-6f4b-5c58-ad19-03b03ef0421d"] ["answer-0f040a93-6f4b-5c58-ad19-03b03ef0421d" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-0f040a93-6f4b-5c58-ad19-03b03ef0421d" :answer/value "$36$ liters"] ["answer-6754d3ae-b8b9-53e9-9b8a-a331a071d537" :answer/id #uuid "6754d3ae-b8b9-53e9-9b8a-a331a071d537"] ["answer-6754d3ae-b8b9-53e9-9b8a-a331a071d537" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-6754d3ae-b8b9-53e9-9b8a-a331a071d537" :answer/value "$4$ liters"] ["answer-897c8d4f-efc3-5482-98f8-433b96884808" :answer/id #uuid "897c8d4f-efc3-5482-98f8-433b96884808"] ["answer-897c8d4f-efc3-5482-98f8-433b96884808" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-897c8d4f-efc3-5482-98f8-433b96884808" :answer/value "$7$ liters"] ["answer-45dc3045-0571-5014-a74d-81cdca7770a0" :answer/id #uuid "45dc3045-0571-5014-a74d-81cdca7770a0"] ["answer-45dc3045-0571-5014-a74d-81cdca7770a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-45dc3045-0571-5014-a74d-81cdca7770a0" :answer/value "$8$ liters"] ["answer-2bd953d5-ff42-579c-acb2-def35c667d27" :answer/id #uuid "2bd953d5-ff42-579c-acb2-def35c667d27"] ["answer-2bd953d5-ff42-579c-acb2-def35c667d27" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-2bd953d5-ff42-579c-acb2-def35c667d27" :answer/value "$9$ liters"] ["answer-field-fd0af01a-73e4-519b-b081-ca37b5a21db0" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-fd0af01a-73e4-519b-b081-ca37b5a21db0" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-fd0af01a-73e4-519b-b081-ca37b5a21db0" :answer-field/correct "answer-2bd953d5-ff42-579c-acb2-def35c667d27"] ["answer-field-fd0af01a-73e4-519b-b081-ca37b5a21db0" :answer-field/choices "answer-0f040a93-6f4b-5c58-ad19-03b03ef0421d"] ["answer-field-fd0af01a-73e4-519b-b081-ca37b5a21db0" :answer-field/choices "answer-6754d3ae-b8b9-53e9-9b8a-a331a071d537"] ["answer-field-fd0af01a-73e4-519b-b081-ca37b5a21db0" :answer-field/choices "answer-897c8d4f-efc3-5482-98f8-433b96884808"] ["answer-field-fd0af01a-73e4-519b-b081-ca37b5a21db0" :answer-field/choices "answer-45dc3045-0571-5014-a74d-81cdca7770a0"] ["answer-field-fd0af01a-73e4-519b-b081-ca37b5a21db0" :answer-field/choices "answer-2bd953d5-ff42-579c-acb2-def35c667d27"] ["question-c922cf86-366d-5b96-b6c8-e7d64dc90ff6" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-fd0af01a-73e4-519b-b081-ca37b5a21db0"] [17592186152104 :knowledge-point/questions "question-c922cf86-366d-5b96-b6c8-e7d64dc90ff6"] ["question-33c6a696-2687-53cf-91b5-ae5c51099777" :question/id #uuid "33c6a696-2687-53cf-91b5-ae5c51099777"] ["question-33c6a696-2687-53cf-91b5-ae5c51099777" :question/math-academy-id "q-67896"] ["question-33c6a696-2687-53cf-91b5-ae5c51099777" :question/problem "At a fair, a carousel makes $3$ revolutions in  $\\frac{3}{5}$ minutes. How many revolutions does it make in $1$ minute?"] ["question-33c6a696-2687-53cf-91b5-ae5c51099777" :question/worked-solution "To find out the number of revolutions the carousel makes in $1$ minute, we can compute the unit rate of revolutions per minute. We do this by dividing the number of revolutions, $3,$ by the number of minutes, $\\frac{3}{5}$, as follows:  \n\n$$\n3÷\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3÷\\frac{3}{5} & =3\\times \\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the carousel makes  $5$ revolutions in $1$ minute."] ["question-33c6a696-2687-53cf-91b5-ae5c51099777" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-b3b22992-4d44-561b-9f44-cf2d5dba8e93" :answer-field/id #uuid "b3b22992-4d44-561b-9f44-cf2d5dba8e93"] ["answer-24c31280-ecb5-58d8-bd23-e18274f8038d" :answer/id #uuid "24c31280-ecb5-58d8-bd