2992-4d44-561b-9f44-cf2d5dba8e93" :answer-field/choices "answer-d14f5c60-8845-5e03-9980-e5ba4292d37b"] ["question-33c6a696-2687-53cf-91b5-ae5c51099777" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-b3b22992-4d44-561b-9f44-cf2d5dba8e93"] [17592186152104 :knowledge-point/questions "question-33c6a696-2687-53cf-91b5-ae5c51099777"] ["question-2fa5b801-b137-5833-adf3-4b3d17f99da2" :question/id #uuid "2fa5b801-b137-5833-adf3-4b3d17f99da2"] ["question-2fa5b801-b137-5833-adf3-4b3d17f99da2" :question/math-academy-id "q-169441"] ["question-2fa5b801-b137-5833-adf3-4b3d17f99da2" :question/problem "Owen takes $\\frac{4}{5}$ hours to do $24$ math exercises. How many exercises can he do in $1$ hour?"] ["question-2fa5b801-b137-5833-adf3-4b3d17f99da2" :question/worked-solution "To find the number of exercises Owen can do in $1$ hour, we compute the unit rate of exercises per hour. We do this by dividing the number of exercises Owen does, $24,$ by the number of hours, $\\frac{4}{5},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n24÷\\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24÷\\frac{4}{5} & =24\\times \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\times 5}{1\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{120}{4} \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Owen can do $30$ exercises in $1$ hour."] ["question-2fa5b801-b137-5833-adf3-4b3d17f99da2" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-c50e92a2-cf53-5b22-b046-a98e26f50b91" :answer-field/id #uuid "c50e92a2-cf53-5b22-b046-a98e26f50b91"] ["answer-3d67f7b2-d4a3-5793-a2e5-0a9c8f9fd22e" :answer/id #uuid "3d67f7b2-d4a3-5793-a2e5-0a9c8f9fd22e"] ["answer-3d67f7b2-d4a3-5793-a2e5-0a9c8f9fd22e" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-3d67f7b2-d4a3-5793-a2e5-0a9c8f9fd22e" :answer/value "$27$ exercises"] ["answer-670bc25b-0858-5ec5-b504-a4960a721849" :answer/id #uuid "670bc25b-0858-5ec5-b504-a4960a721849"] ["answer-670bc25b-0858-5ec5-