954f025453"] ["question-6cccdce8-916f-545e-8523-5e2c1794424a" :question/id #uuid "6cccdce8-916f-545e-8523-5e2c1794424a"] ["question-6cccdce8-916f-545e-8523-5e2c1794424a" :question/math-academy-id "q-67967"] ["question-6cccdce8-916f-545e-8523-5e2c1794424a" :question/problem "Riding his electric bike, Matt can go $\\frac{1}{2}$ of a mile in $\\frac{3}{4}$  minutes. What is the unit rate of miles covered per minute?"] ["question-6cccdce8-916f-545e-8523-5e2c1794424a" :question/worked-solution "To compute the unit rate of miles per minute, we divide the number of miles, $\\frac{1}{2},$ by the number of minutes, $\\frac{3}{4}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}÷\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}÷\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 4}{2\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2}{2\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\times 2}{\\cancel{2}\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $\\frac{2}{3}$ miles per minute."] ["question-6cccdce8-916f-545e-8523-5e2c1794424a" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55" :answer-field/id #uuid "80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55"] ["answer-34d51373-e059-5bb2-b915-ea1bf3300d5d" :answer/id #uuid "34d51373-e059-5bb2-b915-ea1bf3300d5d"] ["answer-34d51373-e059-5bb2-b915-ea1bf3300d5d" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-34d51373-e059-5bb2-b915-ea1bf3300d5d" :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{2}$ mile per minute"] ["answer-08d6df6d-3090-5380-af3f-b6a2c1297623" :answer/id #uuid "08d6df6d-3090-5380-af3f-b6a2c1297623"] ["answer-08d6df6d-3090-5380-af3f-b6a2c1297623" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-08d6df6d-3090-5380-af3f-b6a2c1297623" :answer/value "$\\frac{2}{3}$ miles per minute"] ["answer-d6bb8ba7-cf6b-5bb3-aa7f-0dc97af0d2b1" :answer/id #uuid "d6bb8ba7-cf6b-5bb3-aa7f-0dc97af0d2b1"] ["answer-d6bb8ba7-cf6b-5bb3-aa7f-0dc97af0d2b1" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-d6bb8ba7-cf6b-5bb3-aa7f-0dc97af0d2b1" :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{4}$ miles per minute"] ["answer-4bc6dd10-ce27-56ea-8697-58814869e5a1" :answer/id #uuid "4bc6dd10-ce27-56ea-8697-58814869e5a1"] ["answer-4bc6dd10-ce27-56ea-8697-58814869e5a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-4bc6dd10-ce27-56ea-8697-58814869e5a1" :answer/value "$\\frac{4}{5}$ miles per minute"] ["answer-a26136a8-2a89-5671-9dc1-9250849e70bc" :answer/id #uuid "a26136a8-2a89-5671-9dc1-9250849e70bc"] ["answer-a26136a8-2a89-5671-9dc1-9250849e70bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-a26136a8-2a89-5671-9dc1-9250849e70bc" :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{6}$ miles per minute"] ["answer-field-80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55" :answer-field/correct "answer-08d6df6d-3090-5380-af3f-b6a2c1297623"] ["answer-field-80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55" :answer-field/choices "answer-34d51373-e059-5bb2-b915-ea1bf3300d5d"] ["answer-field-80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55" :answer-field/choices "answer-08d6df6d-3090-5380-af3f-b6a2c1297623"] ["answer-field-80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55" :answer-field/choices "answer-d6bb8ba7-cf6b-5bb3-aa7f-0dc97af0d2b1"] ["answer-field-80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55" :answer-field/choices "answer-4bc6dd10-ce27-56ea-8697-58814869e5a1"] ["answer-field-80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55" :answer-field/choices "answer-a26136a8-2a89-5671-9dc1-9250849e70bc"] ["question-6cccdce8-916f-545e-8523-5e2c1794424a" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55"] [17592186152106 :knowledge-point/questions "question-6cccdce8-916f-545e-8523-5e2c1794424a"] ["question-37159145-3e5f-53f2-822d-4367df7cb7bc" :question/id #uuid "37159145-3e5f-53f2-822d-4367df7cb7bc"] ["question-37159145-3e5f-53f2-822d-4367df7cb7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-67957"] ["question-37159145-3e5f-53f2-822d-4367df7cb7bc" :question/problem "A skydiver falls $\\frac{4}{5}$ of a mile in $\\frac{2}{5}$ minutes. What is the unit rate of miles fallen per minute?"] ["question-37159145-3e5f-53f2-822d-4367df7cb7bc" :question/worked-solution "To compute the unit rate of miles per minute, we divide the number of miles, $\\frac{4}{5},$ by the number of minutes, $\\frac{2}{5}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{2}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\times 5}{5\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2\\times 5}{5\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\times \\cancel{5}\\times 2}{\\cancel{2}\\times \\cancel{5}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $2$ miles per minute."] ["question-37159145-3e5f-53f2-822d-4367df7cb7bc" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840" :answer-field/id #uuid "f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840"] ["answer-a70b8aba-2006-588a-9b36-9664101c1dc6" :answer/id #uuid "a70b8aba-2006-588a-9b36-9664101c1dc6"] ["answer-a70b8aba-2006-588a-9b36-9664101c1dc6" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-a70b8aba-2006-588a-9b36-9664101c1dc6" :answer/value "$2$ miles per minute"] ["answer-d42f7600-375a-59ca-9455-2853caf93570" :answer/id #uuid "d42f7600-375a-59ca-9455-2853caf93570"] ["answer-d42f7600-375a-59ca-9455-2853caf93570" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-d42f7600-375a-59ca-9455-2853caf93570" :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{10}$ miles per minute"] ["answer-70090ca7-e591-51bc-b92a-43641ff66542" :answer/id #uuid "70090ca7-e591-51bc-b92a-43641ff66542"] ["answer-70090ca7-e591-51bc-b92a-43641ff66542" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-70090ca7-e591-51bc-b92a-43641ff66542" :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{2}$ miles per minute"] ["answer-493940a9-253b-5a45-a88e-7e139a8230c4" :answer/id #uuid "493940a9-253b-5a45-a88e-7e139a8230c4"] ["answer-493940a9-253b-5a45-a88e-7e139a8230c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-493940a9-253b-5a45-a88e-7e139a8230c4" :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{10}$ miles per minute"] ["answer-d43afe7e-a4ce-5422-b4f9-1126acce88dd" :answer/id #uuid "d43afe7e-a4ce-5422-b4f9-1126acce88dd"] ["answer-d43afe7e-a4ce-5422-b4f9-1126acce88dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-d43afe7e-a4ce-5422-b4f9-1126acce88dd" :answer/value "$\\frac{4}{5}$ miles per minute"] ["answer-field-f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840" :answer-field/correct "answer-a70b8aba-2006-588a-9b36-9664101c1dc6"] ["answer-field-f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840" :answer-field/choices "answer-a70b8aba-2006-588a-9b36-9664101c1dc6"] ["answer-field-f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840" :answer-field/choices "answer-d42f7600-375a-59ca-9455-2853caf93570"] ["answer-field-f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840" :answer-field/choices "answer-70090ca7-e591-51bc-b92a-43641ff66542"] ["answer-field-f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840" :answer-field/choices "answer-493940a9-253b-5a45-a88e-7e139a8230c4"] ["answer-field-f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840" :answer-field/choices "answer-d43afe7e-a4ce-5422-b4f9-1126acce88dd"] ["question-37159145-3e5f-53f2-822d-4367df7cb7bc" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840"] [17592186152106 :knowledge-point/questions "question-37159145-3e5f-53f2-822d-4367df7cb7bc"] ["question-6aea863a-357f-52de-8ad7-b9c9c32993ae" :question/id #uuid "6aea863a-357f-52de-8ad7-b9c9c32993ae"] ["question-6aea863a-357f-52de-8ad7-b9c9c32993ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-67962"] ["question-6aea863a-357f-52de-8ad7-b9c9c32993ae" :question/problem "A certain satellite makes $\\frac{1}{3}$  of its orbit around the Earth in $\\frac{1}{6}$ of a day. How many times can it orbit Earth in $1$ day?"] ["question-6aea863a-357f-52de-8ad7-b9c9c32993ae" :question/worked-solution "To find how many  times the satellite  can go around the Earth in $1$ day, we can compute the orbital unit rate per day. We do this by dividing the amount of the orbit the satellite travels, $\\frac{1}{3},$ by the corresponding fraction of a day, $\\frac{1}{6},$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{6}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 6}{3\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the satellite can orbit Earth $2$ times in $1$ day."] ["question-6aea863a-357f-52de-8ad7-b9c9c32993ae" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5" :answer-field/id #uuid "3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5"] ["answer-41a64a33-32be-5dd3-a470-cc04307cda2b" :answer/id #uuid "41a64a33-32be-5dd3-a470-cc04307cda2b"] ["answer-41a64a33-32be-5dd3-a470-cc04307cda2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-41a64a33-32be-5dd3-a470-cc04307cda2b" :answer/value "2"] ["answer-506250f5-ba3b-5016-bdc4-5b2f75606b96" :answer/id #uuid "506250f5-ba3b-5016-bdc4-5b2f75606b96"] ["answer-506250f5-ba3b-5016-bdc4-5b2f75606b96" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-506250f5-ba3b-5016-bdc4-5b2f75606b96" :answer/value "3"] ["answer-8d0a7fb2-745a-5a4b-8c76-c522f2f54c2a" :answer/id #uuid "8d0a7fb2-745a-5a4b-8c76-c522f2f54c2a"] ["answer-8d0a7fb2-745a-5a4b-8c76-c522f2f54c2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-8d0a7fb2-745a-5a4b-8c76-c522f2f54c2a" :answer/value "4"] ["answer-663127ee-ca61-54fd-bd7a-e8c3c21cf07f" :answer/id #uuid "663127ee-ca61-54fd-bd7a-e8c3c21cf07f"] ["answer-663127ee-ca61-54fd-bd7a-e8c3c21cf07f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-663127ee-ca61-54fd-bd7a-e8c3c21cf07f" :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["answer-926c20da-babe-5216-892f-00c1074d45bb" :answer/id #uuid "926c20da-babe-5216-892f-00c1074d45bb"] ["answer-926c20da-babe-5216-892f-00c1074d45bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-926c20da-babe-5216-892f-00c1074d45bb" :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}"] ["answer-field-3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5" :answer-field/correct "answer-41a64a33-32be-5dd3-a470-cc04307cda2b"] ["answer-field-3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5" :answer-field/choices "answer-41a64a33-32be-5dd3-a470-cc04307cda2b"] ["answer-field-3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5" :answer-field/choices "answer-506250f5-ba3b-5016-bdc4-5b2f75606b96"] ["answer-field-3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5" :answer-field/choices "answer-8d0a7fb2-745a-5a4b-8c76-c522f2f54c2a"] ["answer-field-3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5" :answer-field/choices "answer-663127ee-ca61-54fd-bd7a-e8c3c21cf07f"] ["answer-field-3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5" :answer-field/choices "answer-926c20da-babe-5216-892f-00c1074d45bb"] ["question-6aea863a-357f-52de-8ad7-b9c9c32993ae" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5"] [17592186152106 :knowledge-point/questions "question-6aea863a-357f-52de-8ad7-b9c9c32993ae"] [17592186152651 :question/worked-solution "To find the number of miles Jim can run in $1$ hour, we can compute the unit rate of miles per hour. We do this by dividing the number of miles, $\\frac{5}{6},$ by the number of hours, $\\frac{1}{5},$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{6}÷\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{6}÷\\frac{1}{5} & =\\frac{5}{6}\\times \\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 5}{6\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{6}=4\\,\\text{R}1=4\\,\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jim can run $4\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ miles in $1$ hour."] [17592186152651 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-d04bb74e-c21f-56f1-9310-a6adde831b6a" :answer/id #uuid "d04bb74e-c21f-56f1-9310-a6adde831b6a"] ["answer-d04bb74e-c21f-56f1-9310-a6adde831b6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-d04bb74e-c21f-56f1-9310-a6adde831b6a" :answer/value "3\\,\\frac{3}{6}"] ["answer-0153e8af-7940-50ee-90ef-b1dfd0e45b5a" :answer/id #uuid "0153e8af-7940-50ee-90ef-b1dfd0e45b5a"] ["answer-0153e8af-7940-50ee-90ef-b1dfd0e45b5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-0153e8af-7940-50ee-90ef-b1dfd0e45b5a" :answer/value "4\\,\\frac{1}{3}"] ["answer-148f688d-3a85-57a5-b966-a8820f8f1a74" :answer/id #uuid "148f688d-3a85-57a5-b966-a8820f8f1a74"] ["answer-148f688d-3a85-57a5-b966-a8820f8f1a74" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-148f688d-3a85-57a5-b966-a8820f8f1a74" :answer/value "4\\,\\frac{1}{6}"] [17592186150413 :answer-field/correct "answer-148f688d-3a85-57a5-b966-a8820f8f1a74"] [17592186150413 :answer-field/choices "answer-d04bb74e-c21f-56f1-9310-a6adde831b6a"] [17592186150413 :answer-field/choices "answer-0153e8af-7940-50ee-90ef-b1dfd0e45b5a"] [17592186150413 :answer-field/choices "answer-148f688d-3a85-57a5-b966-a8820f8f1a74"] [17592186153341 :tutorial/content "A **unit rate** is a ratio of two quantities stated as some amount of one quantity per unit of the other quantity.\n\nFor example, suppose that Carl can run $100$ meters in $20$ seconds. The unit rate is how many meters Carl can run in $1$ second. To compute Carl's unit rate, we divide the number of meters by the number of seconds:\n\n$$\n100 \\div 20 = 5\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $5$ meters per second. In other words, Carl can run $5$ meters in $1$ second.\n\nUnit rates do not always come out to whole numbers. For example, suppose Jamie can run $100$ meters in $15$ seconds. To compute Jamie's unit rate, we again divide the number of meters by the number of seconds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100÷15 & =\\frac{100}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{100÷5}{15÷5} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{3} = 6 \\, \\textrm{R} 2 = 6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jamie can run $6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}$ meters per second."] [17592186152308 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0]] "retractions" [[17592186151914 :question/worked-solution "To compute the unit rate of miles per gallon of gas, we divide the number of miles, $15$, by the number of gallons of gas,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n15 \\div \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15 \\div \\frac{3}{4} &= 15 \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{1} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15 \\cdot 4}{1 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{60}{3} \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $20$ miles per gallon of gas."] [17592186151915 :question/worked-solution "To find the number of tablespoons in $1$ gram of olive oil, we can compute the unit rate of tablespoons per gram olive oil. We do this by dividing the number of tablespoons,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nby the number of grams, $4$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\div 4\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3} \\div 4 &= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 \\cdot 1}{3 \\cdot 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{12}\n$$\n\nof a tablespoon in $1$ gram of olive oil."] [17592186151916 :question/worked-solution "To find the number of miles that Martha can swim in $1$ hour, we can compute the unit rate of miles per hour. We do this by dividing the number of miles,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nby the number of hours,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} \\div \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{4} \\div \\frac{2}{3} &= \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 \\cdot 3}{4 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{8} = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} 1 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Martha can swim\n\n$$\n1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nmiles in $1$ hour."] [17592186150413 :answer-field/choices 17592186150416] [17592186150413 :answer-field/choices 17592186150414] [17592186150413 :answer-field/choices 17592186150415] [17592186153341 :tutorial/content "A **unit rate** is a ratio of two quantities stated as some amount of one quantity per unit of the other quantity.\n\nFor example, suppose that Carl can run $100$ meters in $20$ seconds. The unit rate is how many meters Carl can run in $1$ second. To compute Carl's unit rate, we divide the number of meters by the number of seconds:\n\n$$\n100 \\div 20 = 5\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $5$ meters per second. In other words, Carl can run $5$ meters in $1$ second.\n\nUnit rates do not always come out to whole numbers. For example, suppose Jamie can run $100$ meters in $15$ seconds. To compute Jamie's unit rate, we again divide the number of meters by the number of seconds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n100 \\div 15 &= \\frac{100}{15} \\\\\n&= \\frac{100 \\div 5}{15 \\div 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{3} = 6 \\, \\textrm{R} 2 = 6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jamie can run\n\n$$\n6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nmeters per second."]]}]